A frequency map gives another way to keep track of complements. First, count every value in the list. Then scan again and use a second map to make sure each pair is emitted once, in the same order as the nodes.

Dry run for values `2, 4, 6` and target `8`:
- Counts are `{2: 1, 4: 1, 6: 1}`
- At `2`, its complement is `6`, so `[2, 6]` is added
- At `4`, the complement is also `4`, but only one `4` exists

```javascript
/*
class ListNode {
    constructor(val) {
        this.val = val;
        this.next = null;
        this.prev = null;
    }
}
*/

class Solution {
    findPairsWithGivenSum(head, target) {
        const counts = new Map();
        const used = new Map();
        const result = [];

        let current = head;
        while (current !== null) {
            counts.set(current.val, (counts.get(current.val) || 0) + 1);
            current = current.next;
        }

        current = head;
        while (current !== null) {
            const value = current.val;
            const needed = target - value;
            const available = counts.get(needed) || 0;
            const alreadyUsed = used.get(needed) || 0;
            const currentUsed = used.get(value) || 0;

            if (available > alreadyUsed &&
                (needed !== value || available - alreadyUsed > currentUsed + 1)) {
                result.push([value, needed]);
                used.set(value, currentUsed + 1);
                used.set(needed, (used.get(needed) || 0) + 1);
            }

            current = current.next;
        }

        return result;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` for the two list scans. Space complexity: `O(n)` for the maps. An empty input or a target with no possible complement returns an empty array.

Because the values are sorted, each node can look for its complement in the part of the list after it. A binary search is done by finding the tail first, then moving through the list with slow and fast pointers.

Only the later half of the list is searched for each current node, so a pair is not reported twice. This saves space compared with a hash table, but it takes more time than the two-pointer scan.

```csharp
using System.Collections.Generic;

/*
public class ListNode {
    public int val;
    public ListNode next;
    public ListNode prev;

    public ListNode(int val) {
        this.val = val;
        this.next = null;
        this.prev = null;
    }
}
*/

public class Solution {
    public List<List<int>> FindPairsWithGivenSum(ListNode head, int target) {
        List<List<int>> result = new List<List<int>>();
        if (head == null) {
            return result;
        }

        ListNode tail = head;
        while (tail.next != null) {
            tail = tail.next;
        }

        for (ListNode current = head; current != null; current = current.next) {
            int needed = target - current.val;
            ListNode low = current.next;
            ListNode high = tail;

            while (low != null && high != null &&
                   low != high && low.prev != high) {
                ListNode middle = low;
                ListNode runner = low;
                while (runner != high && runner.next != high) {
                    runner = runner.next;
                    if (runner != high) {
                        middle = middle.next;
                    }
                }

                if (middle.val == needed) {
                    result.Add(new List<int> { current.val, middle.val });
                    break;
                }

                if (middle.val < needed) {
                    low = middle.next;
                } else {
                    high = middle.prev;
                }
            }
        }

        return result;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` with this linked-list binary search, since finding a middle node takes a scan. Space complexity: `O(1)` apart from the returned pairs. The input links are not changed.

The list is sorted, so two pointers are enough. One starts at the first node and the other at the last node. If their sum is too small, move the left pointer forward. If it is too large, move the right pointer backward.

Dry run for `1 <-> 2 <-> 4 <-> 5` and target `6`:
- `1 + 5 = 6`, store `[1, 5]`
- Move both pointers
- `2 + 4 = 6`, store `[2, 4]`

The list is changed only by moving pointers, not by changing any links.

```cpp
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

/*
class ListNode {
public:
    int val;
    ListNode* next;
    ListNode* prev; 

    ListNode(int value) : val(value), next(nullptr), prev(nullptr) {}

    ~ListNode() {
        delete next;
    }
};
*/

class Solution {
public:
    vector<vector<int>> findPairsWithGivenSum(ListNode* head, int target) {
        vector<vector<int>> result;
        if (head == nullptr) {
            return result;
        }

        ListNode* left = head;
        ListNode* right = head;
        while (right->next != nullptr) {
            right = right->next;
        }

        while (left != nullptr && right != nullptr &&
               left != right && left->prev != right) {
            long long sum = static_cast<long long>(left->val) + right->val;

            if (sum == target) {
                result.push_back({left->val, right->val});
                left = left->next;
                right = right->prev;
            } else if (sum < target) {
                left = left->next;
            } else {
                right = right->prev;
            }
        }

        return result;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` because each pointer moves through the list once. Space complexity: `O(1)` apart from the returned pairs.

For a simple baseline, check every node against all nodes after it. Starting the inner pointer at `first.next` prevents using the same node twice. Since the values are read from left to right, each pair is added only once.

For example, with `1, 3, 5` and target `6`, the checks find `(1, 5)` and skip the other combinations. This is slower than the sorted two-pointer method, but it is easy to inspect and does not need a set.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

/*
class ListNode {
    int val;
    ListNode next;
    ListNode prev;

    ListNode(int val) {
        this.val = val;
        this.next = null;
        this.prev = null;
    }
}
*/

class Solution {
    public List<List<Integer>> findPairsWithGivenSum(ListNode head, int target) {
        List<List<Integer>> result = new ArrayList<>();

        for (ListNode first = head; first != null; first = first.next) {
            for (ListNode second = first.next; second != null; second = second.next) {
                long sum = (long) first.val + second.val;
                if (sum == target) {
                    List<Integer> pair = new ArrayList<>();
                    pair.add(first.val);
                    pair.add(second.val);
                    result.add(pair);
                }
            }
        }

        return result;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` from the two nested scans. Space complexity: `O(1)` besides the returned list. Edge cases such as a null head, one node, or no matching pair naturally produce an empty result.

